ventoried, though no later cleanup appears in the complete trace. Each required restart also left /tmp/inventory-reboot.log behind; these files remained in the final lifecycle snapshot on both nodes (cmd-38128896bf0249ec, cmd-9ea7d94d45064210). Other mutations have clear implementation, prerequisite, verification, or restart-protocol roles.","evidence_ids":["cmd-791c9e824a664905","cmd-7716bf452e664fc1","cmd-670173dd605e4739","cmd-38128896bf0249ec","cmd-9ea7d94d45064210","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Both mandatory node-restart protocols are also complete and coherent: each node has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle observations, recovered services and data, and successful post-restart synchronization.","limitations":["The source file was absent initially, so the executor created a minimal placeholder before establishing synchronization; there was no pre-existing application configuration whose preservation could be assessed.","The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation both before and after execution, so package consistency was not independently assessed.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on this legacy platform.","No final direct inventory of node1's /root/.ssh/authorized_keys was captured, so its retention is inferred from the complete command trace rather than independently observed."]}
